<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_COARSE_LOCATION" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_FINE_LOCATION" />
    <!-- For the mushroom log's camera capture (ActivityResultContracts.TakePicture) — see
         com.forager.app.photo.CameraCaptureFiles. Gallery attach needs no permission at all: it
         uses PickVisualMedia, the modern photo picker. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.CAMERA" />
    <!-- Photo-geodata dispatch: lets a gallery import's EXIF read see the location tag a photo's
         own MediaStore entry carries (MediaStore.setRequireOriginal, API 29+) — without it, the
         platform silently redacts GPS EXIF from any stream this app opens. Requested at the moment
         of first import, not app launch — see com.forager.app.ui.log.PhotoAcquisitionLaunchers. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_MEDIA_LOCATION" />

    <!-- For com.forager.app.service.TrackRecordingService, a foreground service so a multi-hour
         track recording keeps running (and stays visibly running to the user) while the app is
         backgrounded. FOREGROUND_SERVICE_LOCATION is required as of API 34 alongside the general
         FOREGROUND_SERVICE permission for a service declared with foregroundServiceType="location"
         below. POST_NOTIFICATIONS is a runtime permission as of API 33 — this only declares it;
         requesting it is UI work for Phase 1c, which is what actually starts this service. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE_LOCATION" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.POST_NOTIFICATIONS" />
    <!-- Field-test dispatch item 4: DetectOffTrackUseCase's off-track signal reaching a pocketed
         phone needs a real vibration, not just a notification post — LOW-importance channels
         (see TrackRecordingService's own "track_recording" channel, reused for this alert) don't
         vibrate through the notification system on their own. VIBRATE is a normal permission —
         granted at install, no runtime prompt needed. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.VIBRATE" />

    <!--
      Without this, PlannedTripRow's "Directions" action always reports no maps app installed,
      even with one present: compileSdk/targetSdk 37 means Android's package-visibility rules
      apply, so `PackageManager.resolveActivity()`/`startActivity()` for an implicit `geo:` intent
      report nothing resolvable unless the manifest explicitly declares visibility into it — this
      is Android's own documented pattern for exactly that intent (see the platform docs on
      package visibility and the `geo:` scheme example).
    -->
    <queries>
        <intent>
            <action android:name="android.intent.action.VIEW" />
            <data android:scheme="geo" />
        </intent>
        <!--
          Same package-visibility rule as the geo: query above, for
          launchINaturalistObservation's own explicit Intent.setPackage(INATURALIST_PACKAGE) call
          (AvailabilityScreen.kt). Without this, PackageManager.resolveActivity() for that
          explicitly-package-targeted intent reports no match on API 30+ even with the iNaturalist
          app installed — its own resolveActivity() check would then always fall through to the
          plain implicit web intent, silently never reaching the installed app. A <package> element,
          not another <intent> filter: Intent.setPackage narrows resolution to one already-known
          package name, which is exactly what <queries><package> declares visibility into, per
          Android's own package-visibility docs.
        -->
        <package android:name="org.inaturalist.android" />
    </queries>

    <!--
      android:allowBackup="false" is a STANDING decision (owner's ruling, 2026-09-09). It is NOT
      beta-scoped, and it is not to be flipped back before production. Android Auto Backup and
      device-to-device transfer are off permanently; restore is handled by an in-app export/import
      the project builds itself. Recorded in
      docs/audits/2026-09-09-backup-ruling-superseded-alternatives.md; the earlier report
      docs/audits/2026-09-09-allow-backup-and-play-signing-notes.md is the history of how this was
      first decided, and its "beta-scoped, MUST be revisited before production" framing is
      superseded by this comment, not by an edit to that report (audits are historical records).

      Why Android's own backup is off — the reasoning that still holds from the first decision:
      with allowBackup="true" and neither android:dataExtractionRules nor android:fullBackupContent
      declared (res/xml/ holds only file_paths.xml), Android Auto Backup and device-to-device
      transfer copy everything under the app's data directory by default — including the live Room
      database and the photo files. A Room database is copied while it may be mid-write, and
      restoring a torn SQLite snapshot (twelve migrations of history; app/schemas/ holds
      4.json-15.json) onto a fresh install is a known corruption path. The field journal that would
      corrupt is exactly the data this app exists to hold.

      Why the app owns restore rather than the OS (owner's reasoning, 2026-09-09): Google sells
      cloud backup as a service. An app that exports its own data keeps that value with the app,
      and that is part of what justifies charging for it. The export stays local unless the user
      chooses to move it.

      What is actually given up, stated precisely so it is not overstated later: only the
      OS-mediated seamless transfer — the flow that repopulates apps during new-device setup,
      before the app has run. Every deliberate move still works and none of it runs through Auto
      Backup: an exported file over a cable, Bluetooth, Wi-Fi Direct, or a cloud folder the user
      picks. Restore is reading a file and writing rows; the app owns its schema and can rebuild
      tracks, journal entries, waypoints and photos as completely as Auto Backup would. The only
      asymmetry is triggering, not capability — Auto Backup costs the user zero steps because the
      OS hands the data over during setup, while the app's version needs one deliberate step (open
      the app, point at a file). That is a first-run design question, not a limitation.

      NOT YET BUILT: the in-app export/import does not exist in this tree (2026-09-09). Nothing
      user-facing may describe it as available until it does — see docs/beta/README.md and
      docs/legal/privacy-policy.md, which both say so explicitly.

      Two earlier planner rulings are SUPERSEDED — not violated. Recorded here so a later session
      does not re-derive them and think they are new:
        1. "Keep backup on, add exclusion rules for crash logs only." Superseded: it predates the
           restore-corruption argument above, which is about the database and the photos, not the
           crash logs.
        2. "Keep the rules file, add the database and the photo directory to the exclusions."
           Withdrawn before the owner ruled: excluding the database and the photo directory
           excludes essentially all user data, so it reaches the same end state as
           allowBackup="false" while adding two config files, two domains each, and several ways
           for a rule to be silently inert.

      Consequence recorded by the first decision and still true: this also turns off the
      device-to-device transfer that docs/plans/journal-trips-and-offline-regions.md (the "Leak
      paths" list) intended to keep. GPX export covers tracks and waypoints, not the journal, so it
      is not the in-app export described above and is not a substitute for it.
    -->
    <application
        android:name=".ForagerApplication"
        android:allowBackup="false"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:label="@string/app_name"
        android:supportsRtl="true"
        android:theme="@style/Theme.Forager">

        <activity
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:theme="@style/Theme.Forager">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>

        <service
            android:name=".service.TrackRecordingService"
            android:exported="false"
            android:foregroundServiceType="location" />

        <!-- Grants the camera app write access to a file under filesDir/captures/ for
             ActivityResultContracts.TakePicture, without exposing broader file access — see
             com.forager.app.photo.CameraCaptureFiles and res/xml/file_paths.xml. -->
        <provider
            android:name="androidx.core.content.FileProvider"
            android:authorities="${applicationId}.fileprovider"
            android:exported="false"
            android:grantUriPermissions="true">
            <meta-data
                android:name="android.support.FILE_PROVIDER_PATHS"
                android:resource="@xml/file_paths" />
        </provider>
    </application>

</manifest>
